From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:16:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Makefile: Drop the hardcoded -I/usr/include/protobuf-c

On Linux (and every other non-Darwin Unix) the Makefile sets
INCLUDES = -I/usr/include/protobuf-c unconditionally. The include is
not needed: every source, and the googlechat.pb-c.h that protoc-c
generates, uses <protobuf-c/protobuf-c.h>, which the include directory
that pkg-config reports for libprotobuf-c (or the fallback in
PROTOBUF_CFLAGS) already resolves.

It does break cross-compiling. A cross toolchain built with
poison-system-directories, as in Yocto/OpenEmbedded, rejects the host
path outright:

    cc1: error: include location "/usr/include/protobuf-c" is unsafe
    for cross-compilation [-Werror=poison-system-directories]

Packagers can only work around it by overriding INCLUDES on the make
command line, which also throws away the -ldl the Linux branch appends
to it. Drop the path and leave INCLUDES empty there instead, so the
-ldl is kept.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 Makefile | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
index 8a9b4c0..12ab146 100644
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -50,7 +50,7 @@ else
 
     CC ?= gcc
   else
-    INCLUDES = -I/usr/include/protobuf-c
+    INCLUDES =
     CC ?= gcc
 	ifeq ($(UNAME_S), Linux)
 		INCLUDES += -ldl
-- 
